WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.617
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.917 --> 00:00:09.211
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:16.598
Unit twenty-four: Letters to the Editor. Today you will write formal letters with 'I am writing to' and purpose infinitives.

4
00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:19.539
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.849
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:25.475
Letter.

7
00:00:26.483 --> 00:00:30.201
Letter means a written message sent to a person or a newspaper.

8
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:32.770
The letter appeared on page ten.

9
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:35.873
Your turn. Say: Letter.

10
00:00:38.733 --> 00:00:39.758
Letter.

11
00:00:40.517 --> 00:00:41.413
Editor.

12
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:45.809
Editor means the person in charge of what a newspaper prints.

13
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:48.823
The editor chose three letters for the weekend.

14
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:51.535
Your turn. Say: Editor.

15
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:55.227
Editor.

16
00:00:55.983 --> 00:00:56.950
Opinion.

17
00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.844
Opinion means what you think or believe about something.

18
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.264
Everyone may share an opinion.

19
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:06.735
Your turn. Say: Opinion.

20
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:10.520
Opinion.

21
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:12.064
Reader.

22
00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:16.280
Reader means a person who reads a newspaper or book.

23
00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.929
A reader wrote to praise the story.

24
00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:21.821
Your turn. Say: Reader.

25
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:25.645
Reader.

26
00:01:26.383 --> 00:01:27.303
Respond.

27
00:01:28.317 --> 00:01:30.990
Respond means to answer or react to something.

28
00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:33.397
Please respond within one week.

29
00:01:33.983 --> 00:01:36.578
Your turn. Say: Respond.

30
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:40.434
Respond.

31
00:01:41.183 --> 00:01:42.405
Concise.

32
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:46.786
Concise means short and clear, using few words.

33
00:01:47.383 --> 00:01:49.476
A concise letter is easy to read.

34
00:01:50.083 --> 00:01:52.666
Your turn. Say: Concise.

35
00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.675
Concise.

36
00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:58.267
Argument.

37
00:01:59.283 --> 00:02:02.072
Argument means reasons used to support an idea.

38
00:02:02.683 --> 00:02:05.136
A clear argument persuades readers.

39
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.205
Your turn. Say: Argument.

40
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:11.928
Argument.

41
00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:13.580
Publish.

42
00:02:14.583 --> 00:02:17.941
Publish means to print or put something online for the public.

43
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:20.828
The paper will publish the best letters.

44
00:02:21.417 --> 00:02:24.069
Your turn. Say: Publish.

45
00:02:26.933 --> 00:02:27.818
Publish.

46
00:02:28.633 --> 00:02:31.994
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:35.372
Visal, how should I start a letter to the editor?

48
00:02:36.483 --> 00:02:44.314
Write 'Dear Editor,' and then 'I am writing to respond to your article on road safety.' The infinitive shows your purpose.

49
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:46.766
What comes next?

50
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:57.445
State your opinion, give one clear argument with evidence, and end by asking for action: 'I hope the authorities will respond.'

51
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.724
Why is it important to be concise?

52
00:03:01.817 --> 00:03:08.254
Editors have little space. A concise letter with a strong argument is more likely to be published.

53
00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.040
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:11.883 --> 00:03:14.916
Grammar focus: Purpose: I am writing to plus verb.

55
00:03:15.216 --> 00:03:16.299
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:16.983 --> 00:03:20.132
I am writing to respond to your article on road safety.

57
00:03:21.017 --> 00:03:22.889
I would like to share my opinion.

58
00:03:23.783 --> 00:03:25.713
Readers write to ask for action.

59
00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:35.925
Use 'to + base verb' to show purpose: I am writing to complain, to respond, to suggest. Do not use 'for + verb': not 'I write for complain'.

60
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:40.555
Be careful! Don't say: I write for complain about noise.

61
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:45.335
Say: I am writing to complain about noise.

62
00:03:45.635 --> 00:03:47.728
Use to + verb for purpose.

63
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:51.174
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:51.967 --> 00:03:53.072
Dear Editor.

65
00:03:53.372 --> 00:03:56.997
A letter to the editor lets a reader respond to an article in public.

66
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:03.392
The writer begins by saying, 'I am writing to share my opinion on your report about plastic waste.'

67
00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:08.292
A concise letter then gives one clear argument supported by an example.

68
00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:12.263
It ends politely and asks the authorities to respond.

69
00:04:13.267 --> 00:04:16.195
If the editor likes the letter, the paper may publish it.

70
00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.337
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:22.183 --> 00:04:24.450
I am writing blank respond to your article.

72
00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.579
A, to. B, for. C, that.

73
00:04:31.133 --> 00:04:32.924
The answer is A: to.

74
00:04:34.083 --> 00:04:35.967
Short and clear writing is blank.

75
00:04:36.267 --> 00:04:39.816
A, concise. B, published. C, rural.

76
00:04:43.367 --> 00:04:45.343
The answer is A: concise.

77
00:04:46.483 --> 00:04:48.785
To print something for the public is to blank it.

78
00:04:49.085 --> 00:04:52.332
A, publish. B, respond. C, argue.

79
00:04:55.867 --> 00:04:57.669
The answer is A: publish.

80
00:04:58.817 --> 00:05:00.410
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:07.568
A, I write for complain about noise.. B, I am writing to complain about noise.. C, I am writing complain noise..

82
00:05:11.133 --> 00:05:14.468
The answer is B: I am writing to complain about noise..

83
00:05:15.617 --> 00:05:19.776
According to the reading, a letter ends politely and asks the authorities to…

84
00:05:20.076 --> 00:05:24.402
A, respond. B, resign. C, publish.

85
00:05:27.967 --> 00:05:30.187
The answer is A: respond.

86
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.016
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.488
What issue would you write a letter to the editor about?

88
00:05:47.283 --> 00:05:48.900
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:49.200 --> 00:06:06.776
I would write a letter about plastic waste in rivers. I am concerned because it harms fish and spoils the beauty of our country. In the letter, I would give one clear argument and one example from my town. I would also ask the authorities to respond with a recycling plan.

90
00:06:07.076 --> 00:06:08.785
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:09.683 --> 00:06:15.169
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and purpose: i am writing to plus verb.

92
00:06:15.469 --> 00:06:21.139
Today's value is civic responsibility. Take care of your city, because it is everyone's home.

93
00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:24.463
See you in Unit twenty-five. Goodbye!

94
00:06:24.763 --> 00:06:27.067
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

